1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,906
In the previous topic we narrowed a broad
area down to a question.

2
00:00:06,026 --> 00:00:08,475
Now we ask a harder thing.

3
00:00:08,595 --> 00:00:11,044
Can the question actually be answered?

4
00:00:11,164 --> 00:00:13,613
An answerable question has clear terms,

5
00:00:13,733 --> 00:00:17,407
relies on evidence that exists or can be
collected,

6
00:00:17,527 --> 00:00:22,833
and has a scope that fits the time and
resources you really have.

7
00:00:23,453 --> 00:00:26,310
Every important word in your question
needs

8
00:00:26,430 --> 00:00:29,287
a definition that someone else could
apply.

9
00:00:29,407 --> 00:00:34,305
If your question mentions wellbeing, what
exactly do you mean by it,

10
00:00:34,425 --> 00:00:37,690
and how would two different researchers
recognise it?

11
00:00:37,810 --> 00:00:41,892
An operational definition turns an
abstract idea into something observable.

12
00:00:42,012 --> 00:00:46,910
Without one, two studies of the same
question can reach opposite answers.

13
00:00:47,530 --> 00:00:49,162
Next, think about evidence.

14
00:00:49,282 --> 00:00:55,405
Some questions are perfectly clear but
impossible to answer because the data cannot be collected,

15
00:00:55,525 --> 00:00:57,566
or cannot be collected ethically.

16
00:00:57,686 --> 00:01:01,359
Others can be answered from data that
already exists,

17
00:01:01,479 --> 00:01:04,336
which saves a great deal of time.

18
00:01:04,456 --> 00:01:10,579
Ask where the evidence would come from
before you fall in love with the question.

19
00:01:11,199 --> 00:01:14,056
Scope is where many good questions fail.

20
00:01:14,176 --> 00:01:19,482
A question about every school in the
country may be answerable in principle,

21
00:01:19,602 --> 00:01:23,276
but not by one researcher in a few months.

22
00:01:23,396 --> 00:01:27,069
Be honest about your time, your access to
participants,

23
00:01:27,189 --> 00:01:30,862
and the skills you already have or can
learn.

24
00:01:30,982 --> 00:01:33,431
Descriptive questions ask what is
happening.

25
00:01:33,551 --> 00:01:35,184
Causal questions ask why.

26
00:01:35,304 --> 00:01:38,978
Causal questions are usually much harder
to answer well,

27
00:01:39,098 --> 00:01:42,363
because you have to rule out other
explanations.

28
00:01:42,483 --> 00:01:47,381
Neither kind is better, but they call for
very different research designs.

29
00:01:48,001 --> 00:01:51,674
To finish, run your question through a
quick checklist.

30
00:01:51,794 --> 00:01:53,427
Are the terms defined?

31
00:01:53,547 --> 00:01:57,220
Do you know where the evidence will come
from?

32
00:01:57,340 --> 00:01:59,789
Does the scope fit your resources?

33
00:01:59,909 --> 00:02:03,583
Do you know whether it is descriptive or
causal?

34
00:02:03,703 --> 00:02:10,233
If you can answer yes to all four, your
question is ready for the next stage.
